course: everyday
lesson: 321
title: Helping in the kitchen, bathroom privacy, screen time, playing fair and growing at your own speed
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Helping with the washing up or loading the dishwasher is a real help at home, but sharp knives and very hot water can hurt, so ask a grown-up to deal with those.
explain: Your body and your time on the toilet are private: close the bathroom door, and others should knock before they come in, just as you knock on a closed bathroom door.
explain: Too much time on screens can leave you grumpy and can make it harder to fall asleep, so take breaks, go outside and keep to the limits your family agreed.
explain: In a game, cheating spoils the fun, and people stop wanting to play with a cheat, so play fairly even when you are losing.
explain: Everyone grows at their own speed: some children grow tall early and some later, and both are normal, so teasing someone about it is unkind and wrong.
explain: Example: a girl in Denver feels cross and snappy after three hours of a tablet game. Her mum suggests a bike ride, and an hour later she feels much better.
fact: Helping with the washing up or loading the dishwasher is useful work at home.
fact: Ask a grown-up for help with sharp knives, because they can cut you badly.
fact: Ask a grown-up for help with very hot water, because it can scald your skin.
fact: Close the door when you use the bathroom, because your body and toilet time are private.
fact: Knock on a closed bathroom door and wait, and others should do the same for you.
fact: Too much screen time can make you grumpy and can make sleep worse.
fact: Taking breaks from screens and playing outside helps your mood.
fact: Keeping to the screen limits your family agreed is part of being trusted.
fact: Cheating spoils a game, and people do not want to play with someone who cheats.
fact: Playing fairly matters most when you are losing.
fact: Bodies grow and change at different times, and growing early or late are both normal.
fact: Teasing someone for growing faster or slower than others is unkind.
exercise: You are helping with the washing up and there is a big sharp knife in the sink. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up to wash the knife | Feel around in the bubbles for it | Wash it quickly before anyone sees | Leave it hidden under the water
exercise: Why should a child ask for help with sharp knives and very hot water in the kitchen? | They can cut or burn you badly | Grown-ups like to wash the knives and pans themselves, their own way | Knives get blunt if children wash them | Hot water makes plates break
exercise: Is helping to load the dishwasher a useful job for a child? | Yes, it really helps the family | No, only grown-ups can help in the kitchen | Only if the child is paid for it | Only on birthdays and holidays
exercise: You are using the bathroom at home. What is the usual thing to do? | Close the door, because it is private | Leave the door wide open | Lock everyone else out of the house | Invite your friends in to chat
exercise: The bathroom door is closed and you need something inside. What do you do? | Knock and wait for an answer | Walk straight in | Shout and open the door at once | Push the door to check if it is locked
exercise: Why do we keep the bathroom door closed and knock before going in? | Bodies and toilet time are private | It keeps the bathroom warmer | The door must stay closed by law | It stops the soap drying out
exercise: A boy feels grumpy and snappy after a whole afternoon of video games. What would help most? | Take a break and go outside to play | Play for another two hours to cheer up | Switch to a different screen game | Shout at his sister until he feels better
exercise: What can too much screen time do to you? | Make you grumpy and sleep badly | Make your eyes see in the dark | Make you grow taller | Make you better at every sport
exercise: Your family agreed one hour of tablet a day. What is the right thing to do? | Keep to the hour you agreed | Play secretly at night instead | Keep going if nobody is watching | Use your friend's tablet for more time
exercise: You are losing a board game and could secretly move your piece forward. What do you do? | Play fairly and keep your piece where it is | Move it while nobody is looking | Cheat just once because you are losing | Knock over the board to end the game
exercise: Why is cheating in a game a bad idea? | It spoils the game and people stop wanting to play with you | It is fine as long as you are never caught, and you win more often | It makes the game end faster | It only matters in sports, not board games
exercise: A girl in your class is much taller than everyone else. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own speed, and that is normal | She must be older than the rest of the class | She will stop growing before everyone else for sure | Being tall so early means something must be wrong with her body
exercise: Some children tease a boy for being the shortest in the class. What is true? | Teasing is unkind; he will grow at his own speed | He is short only because he does not eat enough of his dinner | Short children never grow tall | Teasing will help him grow faster
exercise: Why do some children in the same class look much older or younger than others? | Bodies change at different times | Some children are lazier than others | The tallest children always do the most sport | Children grow only in the summer
test: A boy in Leeds offers to dry the dishes. A pan of very hot water is on the side. What should he do? | Let a grown-up empty the hot pan and help with the rest | Empty the hot pan himself quickly to save the grown-up time | Stop helping in the kitchen at all | Pour cold water on his hands first and then lift it
test: A girl's little brother walks into the bathroom without knocking while she is in there. What is a fair thing to say later? | Please knock first, because the bathroom is private | You must never go into the bathroom again while I'm in the house | Doors should always be left open | Nothing, privacy does not matter at home
test: A girl in Sydney is often tired at school and cross at home. She plays on her phone in bed until late. What could help? | Fewer screens, especially before bed | A brighter phone screen at night | More games so she feels happier | Playing standing up instead of lying down
test: Which is true about screen time? | Breaks and outdoor play are good for your mood and sleep | More screen time always makes you happier, as long as the games are fun | Screens before bed help you fall asleep | Screen limits are only for very young children
test: Two friends play cards. One peeks at the other's cards to win. What will probably happen? | The other friend will not want to play with him again | They will both enjoy the game more, because it ends sooner | Nobody minds cheating in card games | He will become better at cards
test: In a race at school, a boy could cut across the grass instead of going round the cones. Nobody would see. What is right? | Run the course properly, because winning by cheating is not real winning | Cut across the grass, because nobody would see and the cones are silly anyway | Cut across, but only for half the race | Stop running so nobody can win
test: A boy in Ohio is the shortest in his year and worries about it. What would a doctor tell him? | Children grow at different speeds, and many grow later | He will stay the shortest for the rest of his life, whatever happens | He needs to stop eating sweets to grow | Only boys who play basketball grow tall
test: Which shows kindness about growing? | Saying nothing unkind about anyone's height or body | Joking about who has grown the most | Calling the smallest child «baby» | Telling a tall girl she looks too old
explain: A boy in England poured boiling water from the kettle to help with tea; the kettle slipped and scalded his hand, and he needed burn dressings for weeks.
explain: A girl in Ohio gamed until late every night; her marks dropped, she was always tired and grumpy, and she fell out with her friends.
fact: Hot water and steam cause many of the burns children get at home.
fact: Too much screen time late at night cuts sleep, and poor sleep makes learning and getting along harder.
test: gonna pour hot water from the kettle for mums tea | Ask an adult to pour it | Pour it fast | Use both hands and look away | Fill it right to the top
test: knife's in the soapy water, gonna fish it out | Ask an adult and never reach for it | Feel around for it | Pull the plug and grab it | Use your fingertips
test: playing till 2am every night, tired all day | Keep to the screen limits and sleep more | Play more to wake up | Drink energy drinks | Sleep in class
test: did u hear a kid poured the boiling kettle himself and "the whole thing tipped on his legs". lesson? | Ask a grown-up for help with very hot water | Kettles are safe to lift once they've switched themselves off | Pour fast so it doesn't spill | Only big pans can scald you
test: my mate says a boy gamed till 4am every night and "fell asleep in a test and got zero". what's the true bit | Late screen time cuts sleep and makes learning harder | Screens help you sleep better | Gaming at night improves marks | Sleep doesn't affect school
test: kids say the tall girl in year 5 "must be lying about her age". pass it on? | No, growing early or late is normal; don't spread it | Yes, she must be older | Yes, and ask her mum | Yes, it's funny and everyone's saying it anyway
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises privacy in the bathroom? | The door is closed, and others knock and wait before entering | The door is kept wide open so that everyone in the family can come and go | Only adults are entitled to privacy | Knocking is required only at night
exam: Identify the principal cause of many burns suffered by children at home. | Hot water and steam | Cold metal | Sunlight through windows | Electric lights
exam: It is given that a family has agreed limits on screen time. Select the response consistent with being trusted. | The child keeps to the limits agreed | The child extends them when unobserved | The child ignores them at weekends | The child negotiates daily for more
exam: State when playing fairly matters most. | When one is losing | When one is winning by a great margin | Only during official matches | Only when an adult is watching
